            'body_en' => "The name is among the oldest still in use anywhere. The tablets of Ebla mention Irbilum around 2300 BC; the kings of Ur fought over Urbilum; to the Assyrians it was Arba-ilu, the city of four gods, whose temple of Ishtar of Arbela was repaired by Shalmaneser, Esarhaddon and Assurbanipal and consulted by kings before their wars. In 331 BC Alexander broke the Persian army at Gaugamela, a day's march away, and the battle has sometimes carried the city's name.\n\nSherds on the slopes go back to the Chalcolithic and perhaps the Neolithic; the natural ground lies thirty-six metres below the present surface. Everything above it is the compressed remains of the settlement itself — mudbrick melted back into earth and built on again — which is why UNESCO calls it, cautiously, one of the oldest continuously inhabited towns in the world.\n\nWhat stands on top today is late Ottoman: an oval of about a hundred houses whose outer walls, pressed together, read from the plain as a single fortress wall. Within it ran a maze of alleys one to two and a half metres wide, around thirty larger courtyard mansions, a hammam of 1775 and the Mulla Afandi mosque. In 2007 the last 840 families were moved out so the fabric could be restored — all but one, left in place so that the chain of habitation would not break.",
